    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d65">

                <pb facs="0138" n="128" sameAs="frus:frus1864p1/pg128"/>

                <head>Mr. Seward to Mr.
                        Adams.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 833.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State,</hi>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1864-02-05"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >February</hi> 5, 1864.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> I enclose copies of the Morning Chronicle,
                    of this city, of yesterday, which contain an article from the Richmond Examiner,
                    of the 25th ultimo, relative to the stranding and destruction of the
                    blockade-runner Vesta, near Wilmington. The article confesses that at least a
                    part of the cargo of the steamer was the property of the insurgent government,
                    so called. You may consequently find it useful as cumulative proof of the fact
                    that their resistance is prolonged by the introduction of such supplies in that
                    way. It is not unlikely that the vessel itself was also really owned by the same
                    party.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>WILLIAM H. SEWARD.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles F. Adams</hi>, Esq., <hi
                            rendition="#italic">&amp;c., &amp;c., &amp;c.</hi></p>
                </postscript>


                <xi:include href="d65-a1.xml" xpointer="d65-a1"/>
            </div>
